 "ticket": "T-0658",
 "lines_ruled_with_an_entry": 27,
 "line_count_note": "27, and it is now measured twice by two methods that do not share a threshold. T-0656 counted 27 off a magnified strip of the TOTAL column. This pass fitted the line grid to the TOTAL column's own ink: 28 ink bands, of which two (centres 769 and 795, 26 px apart against a 76 px line pitch) are the two strokes of one broken figure, giving 27 entries. A straight line through those 27 centres has a pitch of 76.2 px and no residual over 16 px, which is what a correctly counted column of a ruled book looks like. coverage.json carried 31 'to the nearest line' from the contact-sheet pass and is corrected here.",
 "form_note": "The right (continuation) sheet of the 1840 schedule. Columns as printed, left to right: twelve SLAVES columns (six male, six female age bands); TOTAL - number of persons in each family; seven NUMBER OF PERSONS IN EACH FAMILY EMPLOYED IN columns (mining, agriculture, commerce, manufactures and trades, navigation of the ocean, navigation of canals lakes and rivers, learned professions and engineers); PENSIONERS FOR REVOLUTIONARY OR MILITARY SERVICES (ages, names); six DEAF AND DUMB, BLIND, AND INSANE WHITE PERSONS columns; four of the same for coloured persons; and seven SCHOOLS, &c. columns. A blank cell on this form is a zero and is recorded as 0.",
 "right_edge_note": "The form's last column - white persons over 20 years of age who cannot read and write - sits beyond the right edge of the exposure on this image, where the leaf curves into the gutter. It is not recorded here, in either direction: the sheet does not say it is empty.",
 "grid_note": "The column grid is measured off the leaf and not assumed, because these rules LEAN: a darkness profile taken in 300 px bands puts the nine printed rules of the TOTAL-and-industry run at 1004, 1194, 1265, 1329, 1396, 1480, 1564, 1647 and 1712 px at the head of the body and at 1016, 1206, 1275, 1343, 1409, 1492, 1576, 1659 and 1729 at its foot - twelve to seventeen pixels of lean over the leaf, against column widths of 64 to 84. The grid is interpolated between those two measurements, and it is checked against the printed heading before a cell is cut: the column it names `mining` must be the one under the printed word Mining, and the column it names `navigation_canals_lakes_rivers` the one under Navigation of canals, lakes, and rivers. That check is what fixes the two written industry columns as AGRICULTURE and NAVIGATION OF CANALS, LAKES AND RIVERS rather than their neighbours, and the footer row agrees with it: the 25 and the 31 stand under those same two columns. A tenth apparent rule at x=1250, present from y=1200 down and NOT leaning with the others, is a crease in the paper and is excluded on that evidence.",
 "tooling_note": "tools/read_census_continuation.py refuses this leaf outright - 'no industry run bracketed by TOTAL and PENSIONERS: the form is not as expected' - at every --cover from 0.40 to 0.60. The reason is the lean: that tool locates the rules from one profile over the whole body, and a rule that moves 17 px across the leaf smears out of the profile. The measurement above replaces it for this sheet and is recorded here so the next reader does not spend the same passes on it. The tool is not changed by this ticket; the fix it wants is a banded profile, and that is filed rather than slipped in.",
 "closure_note": "The enumerator added his own columns at the foot of the sheet. Those printed totals are transcribed under `footer_as_read` and every committed column is checked against them in `column_closure`. A column that does not close keeps its residual and its reading is NOT adjusted to make the total come out - the residual is the finding.",
 "footer_as_read": {
