 "what_the_sheet_says": "This leaf is a canal-labour sheet, and that is the reading's finding rather than its assumption. Twenty-seven households hold 116 people; twenty-five of them work in agriculture and thirty-one in the navigation of canals, lakes and rivers; not one person on the sheet is returned in commerce, in manufactures and trades, in the learned professions, in mining or in the navigation of the ocean. The last seven lines are what the canal looks like in a schedule: lines 21, 22 and 23 are households of ONE with that one man on the canal, and lines 24, 25, 26 and 27 are households of 11, 7, 7 and 3 with EVERY member of each returned on the canal - eleven of eleven, seven of seven, seven of seven, three of three. Four shanties and three single men, consecutive in the enumerator's walking order. The Illinois and Michigan Canal was under construction through 1840 and this is a page of the men digging it. Nothing here is carried back to 1835: this is LATER EVIDENCE under the domain's own rule, and it names nobody - the names are on a left sheet that has not been found.",
 "the_two_stroke_figure": "Line 24 carries the two-stroke form that this deposit has argued about since T-0627 - two parallel slants that read as 11 or as this hand's 4 - and it carries it TWICE, in the TOTAL column and in the canals column. On this leaf it is settled by arithmetic and not by looking harder. Read as 11 both columns close on the enumerator's own footings EXACTLY, 116 and 31; read as 4 they come to 109 and 24 against footings of 116 and 31. The same leaf also writes a plain 4 on lines 12, 13, 14 and 17 of the TOTAL column, as one cursive glyph and not as two strokes, so on this hand the two forms are distinct and the two-stroke one is 11. That is a witness about THIS hand on THIS leaf. It is not carried to 33S7-9YYJ-5V, -6H or -8D, whose own tickets are open, because a second enumerator's 4 is a second question.",
